    "title": "Grand Rapids native Christina Koch set to become first woman to fly around the moon in Artemis II launch",
    "text": "Roughly 17% of customers in Allegan County were still without power Wednesday evening. ALLEGAN COUNTY, Mich — Crews with Consumers Energy are working around the clock to restore power across West Michigan after severe winds knocked out electricity for tens of thousands, and they’re now preparing for another round of storms. At the height of the outages Tuesday, about 75,000 Consumers Energy customers were power throughout the state. By Wednesday, roughly 40,000 had already been restored, with crews continuing work to bring the remaining customers back online. At 5 p.m. Wednesday evening, roughly 17% of Allegan County was in the dark. Consumers Energy tells 13 ON YOUR SIDE those customers should expect to have their power restored by some point late Wednesday. Most of Plainwell was left without power throughout the morning and afternoon, forcing businesses and residents to improvise. “It was a process this morning to get things going,” David Steffen, owner of Plainwell Auto Supply, who relied on generators to stay open. As neighbors began clearing debris, Consumers Energy deployed a massive response. Roughly 200 Consumers Energy crews were already in the field making repairs by Wednesday morning. About 250 additional contracted crews were sent out Wednesday afternoon, fully stocked with materials to rebuild damaged infrastructure. “We had a massive amount of crews that we planned in advance,” Gregory Salisbury, Vice President of electric distribution engineering for Consumers Energy, told 13 ON YOUR SIDE. Crews are replacing damaged poles, power lines and equipment as quickly as they can. The crews are working 16-hour shifts until restoration is complete. At the same time, the utility is closely watching the forecast. Consumers says it began preparing days in advance, staging crews near areas expected to be hit hardest so they could move in quickly once conditions were safe. Now, with more storms expected, those crews will be ready to adjust as conditions change. “We’ll let the weather pass, then go right back to work,” Salisbury said. Despite the potential for additional damage, Consumers Energy says most customers affected by the initial outages should see power restored quickly, with a majority expected back online by Wednesday night. Officials are urging residents to stay clear of downed power lines and give crews space to work safely as restoration efforts continue.",
